  "figureDialogs": [
    {
      "caption": "NVIDIA explicitly publishes this topology for both DGX H100 and DGX H200. Read the lower GPU/NVSwitch fabric separately from the upper PCIe host and peripheral paths. HBM and CPU DIMMs are not drawn.",
      "id": "h100",
      "image": true,
      "open": true
    },
    {
      "caption": "This is DGX B200, not a GB200 Grace-based tray. The two switches at the bottom serve all eight GPUs. Their separate purple PCIe connection is a control path, not the GPU peer-data fabric. The original image has limited resolution.",
      "id": "b200",
      "image": true,
      "open": true
    },
    {
      "caption": "Use the legend: the GPU-to-ConnectX-8 connection is PCIe Gen6; the CPU-facing path is PCIe Gen5. The many lines beneath the GPUs are the separate NVLink fabric. Memory capacities are not shown; the DGX datasheet and hardware guide disagree on HBM capacity.",
      "id": "b300",
      "image": true,
      "open": true
    },
    {
      "caption": "The upper enclosure is one server. The cable cartridge and nine switch trays below belong to the rack. Light blue means CPU–GPU C2C, pink means CPU-to-CPU CLinks, purple means PCIe, and the thin colored lines below the GPUs are peer NVLink.",
      "id": "gb200",
      "image": true,
      "open": true
    },
    {
      "caption": "This January architecture figure shows component placement, not wiring. Its 14.4 TB/s tray NVLink uses the earlier 3.6 TB/s-per-GPU figure. The current product table used here gives 3 TB/s NVLink and 19.2 TB/s HBM per GPU; the article’s earlier HBM figure is 22 TB/s. “4 TB Fast Memory” combines CPU LPDDR and GPU HBM.",
      "id": "rubin",
      "image": true,
      "open": true
    },
    {
      "caption": "NVIDIA’s 2022 HBM3-era illustration teaches the addressing mechanism. This lesson selects the later 144 GB HBM3e GH200. The diagonal arrows represent address translation/access relationships, not physical cables. “Remote” here means the other processor’s memory inside this superchip.",
      "id": "gh200",
      "image": true,
      "open": true
    },
    {
      "caption": "The two CPU boxes are core clusters within one 20-core CPU. The two tiles are a CPU/system tile and a GPU tile, not two GPUs. The memory-controller block serves the shared physical LPDDR5X; the memory chips themselves are outside this figure.",
      "id": "spark",
      "image": true,
      "open": true
    },
    {
      "caption": "Architecture illustration, not a DGX Station wiring diagram. Its 288 GB / 8 TB/s HBM labels are architecture maxima; this Station has 252 GB / 7.1 TB/s. The peer-NVLink capability shown at left does not establish an NVSwitch fabric inside Station. The PCIe host-interface label describes another GPU capability; this Station’s paired Grace CPU uses C2C. Grace and its 496 GB LPDDR5X are outside this GPU picture.",
      "id": "station",
      "image": true,
      "open": true
    }
  ],
